From ud718c6bb1db28db2 at groups.google.com  Tue Oct 10 09:19:44 2000
From: ud718c6bb1db28db2 at groups.google.com (Steve Buckland)
Date: Tue, 10 Oct 2000 09:19:44 +0000
Subject: Re: Variance of n
Message-ID: <VMZFKM2bZO4.KnNi8lGDGGkJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <VMZFKM2bZO4.-NUwjlepCTMJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
References: <VMZFKM2bZO4.-NUwjlepCTMJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/VMZFKM2bZO4/m/KnNi8lGDGGkJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> > On a basic level, I do not actually understand how n can have a variance,
> > since it is not estimated. However n/L (encounter rate) does certainly have
> > a variance. My assumption has been that var(n) can be calculated by scaling
> > the SE of n/L by L, then squaring. But perhaps I am wrong about this?

Daniel, the material you need is on p90 of the book.  Note that you need a
weighted sample variance of encounter rate, weighted by line length.  As n
is just a constant (L) times the encounter rate, clearly it can have a
variance.  It is an estimator of the expected sample size, E(n) (see for
example p87, 105, 116.

With best wishes,

Steve
